1.21.0: the format crop switch, pushes to +4 and pulls to -3

Every camera with a format of its own (the 126 square, the 1.03:1
instant print, the Super 8 and Academy gates) used to cut that format
from the middle of the input, losing the edges of a 3:2 or 4:3 frame
with no way around it. --crop full keeps the whole input at its own
aspect; an instant camera then puts its border around the full frame.
--crop camera is the default and renders as before. The page has a
Format control in the camera group, labelled with the camera's format
and disabled for cameras that never cropped; the recipe carries it as
crop=. CROP= in super8ify.

--develop now takes push+N and pull-N for any stop count from -3 to
+4, fractions included (push+1.5), plus cross+N for a pushed cross
process; pj_develop_parse/pj_develop_format in the core are the one
reader and writer of that text for the CLI, superjuice, the recipe
reader, the server and the wasm. Within -1..+2 the response is the
linear one of 1.20 and renders byte-identically; each stop beyond
counts 0.6 of one (push_effective in the pipeline, shared by the
development stage and both grain models), so a third and fourth stop
of push buy less contrast than the first two while the grain keeps
coarsening. The page's Developing list runs pull -3 .. push +4 and
cross, cross +1, cross +2; a fractional stop read from a recipe is
added to the list.

The strip shows the decoder's reason in place of a thumbnail the server
could not make (a raw the decoder refuses), rather than a broken icon.

Tests: core (develop text round trips including refusals, monotone and
diminishing push response, the crop switch on super8, polaroid-600 and
a no-crop camera, the recipe keys), compat (crop=full and
develop=push+3.5 round-trip through the recipe, out-of-range refused),
serve (crop and develop through the query, format labels in the camera
list), wasm (push+4, push+5 refused, crop=full in the embedded recipe,
format labels), page (the Format control follows the camera; the
recipe and CLI line carry the new keys).

